────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

/bg kill and quitting yoyo now send SIGTERM to the job's process group, wait up to 2 seconds, then SIGKILL whatever is left. Committed as 4bb59ddf. fmt, clippy with -D warnings, build and the full cargo test all pass with 0 failures.

What changed
• `src/tools.rs`: GroupKill gained signal(sig) -> bool. It sends a signal without disarming, and signal(0) checks whether any process in the group is still alive. kill_now and Drop are unchanged, so the model's bash timeout/cancel is still a plain SIGKILL, as the issue asked.
• `src/commands_bg.rs`: kill() and a new stop_running() go through one shared path, terminate_groups. It sends SIGTERM to every group, waits up to 2 seconds (one window shared by all jobs), then SIGKILLs the survivors. The wait is async so the job's own task can reap the shell during it; otherwise every stop would wait the full 2 seconds. /bg list shows how the job really ended: -15 (SIGTERM), -9 (SIGKILL), or the job's own exit code if its cleanup trap exits normally.
• `src/repl.rs`: every way out of the REPL (/quit, Ctrl-D, double Ctrl+C) calls stop_running(). When it stopped something it prints stopped N running /bg job(s), since quitting can now pause for up to 2 seconds. This file wasn't in the plan's file list, but it is where the quit path lives.

Tests (real processes, in src/commands_bg_kill_tests.rs)
• A plain sleep job ends with -15 and doesn't wait out the 2 seconds.
• A job that traps TERM writes its cleanup marker and keeps its own exit code, 7.
• A job that ignores TERM, and its grandchild, are both killed with SIGKILL after the wait, so the process-group behaviour from #1077 still holds.
• A job that already finished keeps its exit code and gets no signal.
• Two TERM-ignoring jobs under stop_running stop in well under twice the wait.
• I also checked that the tests can fail. Sending KILL in place of TERM made the three TERM tests fail, and giving each job its own wait made the shared-wait test fail. I ran each check as one change-test-restore command, one after the other, and both were restored.

One deviation from the plan: the plan said to leave the existing #1077 tests unedited, but bg_kill_reports_the_signal_not_success asserted the old SIGKILL-only behaviour: plain sleep 30 ends with -9. Under the new behaviour that job ends with -15, so the test could not stay green unedited. I changed only its command, to trap '' TERM; sleep 30. Its -9 assertion is unchanged and still covers the SIGKILL fallback; the commit explains this.

Docs: docs/src/usage/commands.md now describes the TERM → 2 s → KILL order for /bg kill and for quitting. The ARCHITECTURE.md entries for commands_bg.rs and tools.rs are updated, and the old "deliberately SIGKILL only" sentence is marked as superseded rather than deleted.

"Fixes #1080" is accurate: both /bg kill and quit shipped, and the issue itself keeps the bash timeout/cancel on plain SIGKILL. src/tools.rs and src/repl.rs are now about 10 and 20 lines over their recorded sizes in tests/module_size.rs. That is within the allowed margin, so the size test passes, but I didn't update those recorded sizes.
